          "secNumber": "1.1",
          "heading": "Learning objectives",
          "content": "A virus is best understood by distinguishing an extracellular object, a genetic information system and a series of contingent host interactions. Physical structure and classification provide useful labels, but neither determines human risk without substantial additional evidence. This unit connects matter, information and nomenclature while establishing measurement boundaries.\n\nThe five conceptual themes are **definition versus observation; structure and function; taxonomy versus ecology; unit of measurement; uncertainty in classification**. A reader completing this unit should be able to explain each of its five topics, compare at least two rival causes of a given observation, reproduce five quantitative answers, and critique a synthetic research design. Scientific maturity requires distinguishing the scale at which a fact has been measured from the scale at which an explanation is being proposed. If an observation describes nucleic-acid fragments, do not call it an infection count. If an outcome describes an immune-related marker, do not call it proven protection without validation.",
