          "secNumber": "1.5",
          "heading": "Topic 1.2 \u2014 Capsids, envelopes and virion architecture",
          "content": "### Mechanism and explanatory scope\n\nCapsid proteins organize viral genomes into structures that may have icosahedral, helical or more complex symmetries. Some particles also possess host-derived lipid envelopes containing viral proteins. Physical form affects attachment, environmental persistence and exposure of structural epitopes, but virions are highly diverse.\n\nFor **structure and function**, identify the unit of observation, the relevant timescale, and the step for which evidence is missing. A structural observation cannot alone prove a clinical or population-level effect.\n\n### Competing explanations\n\nA structural diagram is not evidence that every particle has the same stability or survives identical environmental conditions. Envelope status is associated with physicochemical differences, yet temperature, matrix, humidity and specific molecular composition strongly modify real outcomes.\n\nCompare a biological change with a shifted sampling frame or instrument response. Connect the topic to **what counts as a virus?** and state one observation that would differentiate the explanations.\n\n### Measurement and controls\n\nUse prepublished structural schematics or synthetic geometric models to compare symmetry and surface-to-volume ratios. Annotate scale bars, imaging uncertainty and the difference between an ideal capsid geometry and an experimentally resolved heterogeneous specimen.\n\nDeclare the outcome, unit, denominator, window and negative control. Technical repeats are not independent biological replicates; no fictional curve supplies a confidence interval or causal proof.\n\n### Limitations and connections\n\nDo not infer the infectious dose of any virus from geometry. Visualized particles may include defective or noninfectious material, and microscopy without appropriate controls cannot establish viability.\n\nConnect the topic to **viral genome diversity and baltimore groups** on an evidence ladder from molecules to populations. Name the missing assumption before moving between scales, and keep clinical or biosafety decisions outside this teaching model.\n\n### Worked quantitative case 1.2\n\n**Question.** A capsid diagram has 12 vertices and 30 edges. Euler\u2019s relation gives how many faces?\n\n**Invented inputs:** `v=12`, `e=30`. **Assumed relation:** `2-v+e`. **Answer:** **20 faces**. Work through the substitution without confusing a percentage with a unitless fraction. State the denominator, and check whether any rate also needs a time unit. A direct ratio answers the arithmetic question but cannot, by itself, establish infectiousness, clinical disease or transmission.\n\n**What might break the inference?** Identify whether a changed sampling frame, a poorly calibrated measurement or an invalid modeling assumption could change the result. Alter the first numerical input by ten percent, keeping other values fixed, and independently recompute the output. Explain whether its response is proportional or nonlinear. All values here are fictional; no measurements or biological replicates were collected.\n\n### Apply the idea\n\n1. Define the central term in **capsids, envelopes and virion architecture** and name one tempting but unsupported inference.\n2. State two explanations of the same imaginary observation and propose a distinguishing, safe, noninfectious analysis.\n3. Recalculate the numerical case with correctly labeled units and discuss a limiting case.\n4. Create a three-column evidence ledger: direct observations, inferred mechanism and absent evidence.\n5. Explain how this topic connects to **viral genome diversity and baltimore groups** while identifying one uncertain link.",
