          "secNumber": "1.7",
          "heading": "Topic 1.4 \u2014 ICTV taxonomy, strains and naming conventions",
          "content": "### Mechanism and explanatory scope\n\nThe International Committee on Taxonomy of Viruses maintains a changing formal hierarchy for virus taxa. Taxonomic species, colloquial virus names, isolates, strains and clinically named variants are not interchangeable. Current formal names and placements should be checked against the latest master species list before publication.\n\nFor **unit of measurement**, identify the unit of observation, the relevant timescale, and the step for which evidence is missing. A structural observation cannot alone prove a clinical or population-level effect.\n\n### Competing explanations\n\nThe name most familiar from the news may describe a disease, a virus, or a historical group rather than the currently approved species taxon. A lineage assignment is an inference from specified evidence; it is not a standalone laboratory diagnosis.\n\nCompare a biological change with a shifted sampling frame or instrument response. Connect the topic to **viral genome diversity and baltimore groups** and state one observation that would differentiate the explanations.\n\n### Measurement and controls\n\nConstruct a metadata table with columns for virus common name, taxon rank, release date, reference identifier and uncertainty. Compare an older scheme against the MSL41 release without claiming the nomenclature will remain fixed.\n\nDeclare the outcome, unit, denominator, window and negative control. Technical repeats are not independent biological replicates; no fictional curve supplies a confidence interval or causal proof.\n\n### Limitations and connections\n\nTaxonomy is revised as evidence accumulates. Avoid interpreting a name change as proof of a sudden biological transformation; reclassification may occur without any observed change in an organism.\n\nConnect the topic to **host range, tropism and ecological specificity** on an evidence ladder from molecules to populations. Name the missing assumption before moving between scales, and keep clinical or biosafety decisions outside this teaching model.\n\n### Worked quantitative case 1.4\n\n**Question.** A classroom taxonomy checklist has 21 correct entries out of 24. What percent are correct?\n\n**Invented inputs:** `k=21`, `n=24`. **Assumed relation:** `100*k/n`. **Answer:** **87.5 %**. Work through the substitution without confusing a percentage with a unitless fraction. State the denominator, and check whether any rate also needs a time unit. A direct ratio answers the arithmetic question but cannot, by itself, establish infectiousness, clinical disease or transmission.\n\n**What might break the inference?** Identify whether a changed sampling frame, a poorly calibrated measurement or an invalid modeling assumption could change the result. Alter the first numerical input by ten percent, keeping other values fixed, and independently recompute the output. Explain whether its response is proportional or nonlinear. All values here are fictional; no measurements or biological replicates were collected.\n\n### Apply the idea\n\n1. Define the central term in **ictv taxonomy, strains and naming conventions** and name one tempting but unsupported inference.\n2. State two explanations of the same imaginary observation and propose a distinguishing, safe, noninfectious analysis.\n3. Recalculate the numerical case with correctly labeled units and discuss a limiting case.\n4. Create a three-column evidence ledger: direct observations, inferred mechanism and absent evidence.\n5. Explain how this topic connects to **host range, tropism and ecological specificity** while identifying one uncertain link.",
