          "secNumber": "1.8",
          "heading": "Topic 1.5 \u2014 Host range, tropism and ecological specificity",
          "content": "### Mechanism and explanatory scope\n\nHost range concerns organisms in which some level of infection can occur; tissue tropism concerns the cell types or organs favored in a particular host context. Receptor availability, intracellular compatibility, immunity, anatomical access and ecology constrain these relationships. Entry is necessary for some pathways but is not by itself a demonstration of productive infection.\n\nFor **uncertainty in classification**, identify the unit of observation, the relevant timescale, and the step for which evidence is missing. A structural observation cannot alone prove a clinical or population-level effect.\n\n### Competing explanations\n\nA cultured cell expressing an attachment receptor may bind particles while lacking the cellular environment for replication. Conversely, experimental uptake by an artificial cell model does not establish natural circulation among a species in the wild.\n\nCompare a biological change with a shifted sampling frame or instrument response. Connect the topic to **ictv taxonomy, strains and naming conventions** and state one observation that would differentiate the explanations.\n\n### Measurement and controls\n\nDraw a causal diagram separating contact, entry, replication, shedding and onward transmission. Evaluate a fictional host-range matrix as an inference problem, identifying where an unmeasured stage makes a positive signal ambiguous.\n\nDeclare the outcome, unit, denominator, window and negative control. Technical repeats are not independent biological replicates; no fictional curve supplies a confidence interval or causal proof.\n\n### Limitations and connections\n\nDo not rank real species as transmission sources on receptor similarities alone. Host competence requires additional data and field context, and statements about zoonotic risk demand careful evidence.\n\nConnect the topic to **what counts as a virus?** on an evidence ladder from molecules to populations. Name the missing assumption before moving between scales, and keep clinical or biosafety decisions outside this teaching model.\n\n### Worked quantitative case 1.5\n\n**Question.** A conceptual host matrix shows 15 positive entry observations but only 4 replication-consistent outcomes. What conditional fraction of those entry observations is replication-consistent?\n\n**Invented inputs:** `p=4`, `e=15`. **Assumed relation:** `p/e`. **Answer:** **0.266667 fraction**. Work through the substitution without confusing a percentage with a unitless fraction. State the denominator, and check whether any rate also needs a time unit. A direct ratio answers the arithmetic question but cannot, by itself, establish infectiousness, clinical disease or transmission.\n\n**What might break the inference?** Identify whether a changed sampling frame, a poorly calibrated measurement or an invalid modeling assumption could change the result. Alter the first numerical input by ten percent, keeping other values fixed, and independently recompute the output. Explain whether its response is proportional or nonlinear. All values here are fictional; no measurements or biological replicates were collected.\n\n### Apply the idea\n\n1. Define the central term in **host range, tropism and ecological specificity** and name one tempting but unsupported inference.\n2. State two explanations of the same imaginary observation and propose a distinguishing, safe, noninfectious analysis.\n3. Recalculate the numerical case with correctly labeled units and discuss a limiting case.\n4. Create a three-column evidence ledger: direct observations, inferred mechanism and absent evidence.\n5. Explain how this topic connects to **what counts as a virus?** while identifying one uncertain link.",
