          "content": "Virology spans structural, cellular, ecological and clinical observations. A claim supported at one level may be unjustified at another. Check whether a measured signal is a proxy, a rate or a true outcome. Sampling time, location, group composition and denominators can change interpretation even when the plotted shape looks identical.\n\nThis unit uses models as tools for exploring assumptions. One parameter may be an arbitrary scale, while another represents a mathematical saturation point. Neither value should be mistaken for a calibrated property of a real virus. When interacting with a chart, first predict a direction, then alter one control, then explain the change in terms of the formula. Repeat with the second control and look for a limiting case. Observe how even a simple model can be illuminating for reasoning yet unsuitable for operational decisions. Reading the explicit warnings attached to each model is part of the exercise.",
