          "secNumber": "4.4",
          "heading": "Topic 4.1 \u2014 Cellular sensing and antiviral interferon programs",
          "content": "### Mechanism and explanatory scope\n\nHost cells respond to viral-associated molecular patterns through diverse recognition systems. Interferon signaling can coordinate antiviral states by changing expression of many proteins, but the effects depend on cell type, prior exposure, timing and signal strength. Organism-level outcomes reflect an interacting system of barriers and immune cells.\n\nFor **host context**, identify the unit of observation, the relevant timescale, and the step for which evidence is missing. A structural observation cannot alone prove a clinical or population-level effect.\n\n### Competing explanations\n\nA strong interferon-associated transcript signature can indicate sensing, inflammation or persistent signaling without proving that productive infection is controlled. In some contexts, late or dysregulated inflammation contributes to harm rather than protection.\n\nCompare a biological change with a shifted sampling frame or instrument response. Connect the topic to **host injury, pathology and clinical endpoint separation** and state one observation that would differentiate the explanations.\n\n### Measurement and controls\n\nInterpret an invented time course of a sensing reporter, gene-expression marker and cell viability. Decide which measurements speak to signaling and which might speak to host function; make no conclusion about human symptoms from the toy curves.\n\nDeclare the outcome, unit, denominator, window and negative control. Technical repeats are not independent biological replicates; no fictional curve supplies a confidence interval or causal proof.\n\n### Limitations and connections\n\nThis is not a guide to modulating antiviral immunity in vivo. Abstract signaling curves exclude complex tissue feedback and should never direct patient-specific decisions.\n\nConnect the topic to **intrinsic restriction and host-cell compatibility** on an evidence ladder from molecules to populations. Name the missing assumption before moving between scales, and keep clinical or biosafety decisions outside this teaching model.\n\n### Worked quantitative case 4.1\n\n**Question.** An invented signaling index falls from 64 to 32 in 5 units of time. What is the observed factor?\n\n**Invented inputs:** `a=64`, `b=32`. **Assumed relation:** `a/b`. **Answer:** **2 fold**. Work through the substitution without confusing a percentage with a unitless fraction. State the denominator, and check whether any rate also needs a time unit. A direct ratio answers the arithmetic question but cannot, by itself, establish infectiousness, clinical disease or transmission.\n\n**What might break the inference?** Identify whether a changed sampling frame, a poorly calibrated measurement or an invalid modeling assumption could change the result. Alter the first numerical input by ten percent, keeping other values fixed, and independently recompute the output. Explain whether its response is proportional or nonlinear. All values here are fictional; no measurements or biological replicates were collected.\n\n### Apply the idea\n\n1. Define the central term in **cellular sensing and antiviral interferon programs** and name one tempting but unsupported inference.\n2. State two explanations of the same imaginary observation and propose a distinguishing, safe, noninfectious analysis.\n3. Recalculate the numerical case with correctly labeled units and discuss a limiting case.\n4. Create a three-column evidence ledger: direct observations, inferred mechanism and absent evidence.\n5. Explain how this topic connects to **intrinsic restriction and host-cell compatibility** while identifying one uncertain link.",
