          "secNumber": "4.5",
          "heading": "Topic 4.2 \u2014 Intrinsic restriction and host-cell compatibility",
          "content": "### Mechanism and explanatory scope\n\nCells contain constitutive and induced factors that may block various stages of viral information flow. Such intrinsic restrictions can be linked to nucleic-acid sensing, protein degradation or cellular trafficking. Multiple barriers can act in series, so release from one hypothetical block need not permit full completion of the life cycle.\n\nFor **immune signaling**, identify the unit of observation, the relevant timescale, and the step for which evidence is missing. A structural observation cannot alone prove a clinical or population-level effect.\n\n### Competing explanations\n\nAn association between one host gene and a lower viral-related reporter signal does not demonstrate the gene's direct molecular target. The gene may instead affect cell metabolism, survival, uptake or assay background.\n\nCompare a biological change with a shifted sampling frame or instrument response. Connect the topic to **cellular sensing and antiviral interferon programs** and state one observation that would differentiate the explanations.\n\n### Measurement and controls\n\nDevelop a causal diagram that includes host-cell viability, reporter expression and at least two intermediate states. Propose orthogonal noninfectious reporter controls to separate general toxicity from a pathway-specific effect.\n\nDeclare the outcome, unit, denominator, window and negative control. Technical repeats are not independent biological replicates; no fictional curve supplies a confidence interval or causal proof.\n\n### Limitations and connections\n\nHost genetic variation is not a deterministic susceptibility test. Phenotypes depend on viral systems, tissues and exposure, and simplistic one-gene models often fail externally.\n\nConnect the topic to **antibodies, lymphocytes and immune memory** on an evidence ladder from molecules to populations. Name the missing assumption before moving between scales, and keep clinical or biosafety decisions outside this teaching model.\n\n### Worked quantitative case 4.2\n\n**Question.** A hypothetical abstract reporter is 35 units in one state and 42 in another. What is the relative increase?\n\n**Invented inputs:** `a=35`, `b=42`. **Assumed relation:** `100*(b-a)/a`. **Answer:** **20 %**. Work through the substitution without confusing a percentage with a unitless fraction. State the denominator, and check whether any rate also needs a time unit. A direct ratio answers the arithmetic question but cannot, by itself, establish infectiousness, clinical disease or transmission.\n\n**What might break the inference?** Identify whether a changed sampling frame, a poorly calibrated measurement or an invalid modeling assumption could change the result. Alter the first numerical input by ten percent, keeping other values fixed, and independently recompute the output. Explain whether its response is proportional or nonlinear. All values here are fictional; no measurements or biological replicates were collected.\n\n### Apply the idea\n\n1. Define the central term in **intrinsic restriction and host-cell compatibility** and name one tempting but unsupported inference.\n2. State two explanations of the same imaginary observation and propose a distinguishing, safe, noninfectious analysis.\n3. Recalculate the numerical case with correctly labeled units and discuss a limiting case.\n4. Create a three-column evidence ledger: direct observations, inferred mechanism and absent evidence.\n5. Explain how this topic connects to **antibodies, lymphocytes and immune memory** while identifying one uncertain link.",
