          "secNumber": "4.6",
          "heading": "Topic 4.3 \u2014 Antibodies, lymphocytes and immune memory",
          "content": "### Mechanism and explanatory scope\n\nB cells, antibodies and T cells recognize different antigenic contexts and contribute in distinct ways to protective responses. Neutralization and other antibody-mediated functions are assay specific; memory can alter response speed or quality after previous exposure. Cellular immunity may matter even when a particular antibody measurement is low.\n\nFor **correlates versus outcomes**, identify the unit of observation, the relevant timescale, and the step for which evidence is missing. A structural observation cannot alone prove a clinical or population-level effect.\n\n### Competing explanations\n\nAn antibody-binding value is not the same as a validated correlate of protection. A decline in circulating antibody may coexist with memory cells or tissue-localized immunity, and protection may differ by endpoint such as infection or severe disease.\n\nCompare a biological change with a shifted sampling frame or instrument response. Connect the topic to **intrinsic restriction and host-cell compatibility** and state one observation that would differentiate the explanations.\n\n### Measurement and controls\n\nUse a constructed longitudinal graph with antibody concentration and memory-cell proxy on explicitly different axes. Calculate a simple half-life for an illustrative signal while clearly noting that real biological decay may be multiphasic.\n\nDeclare the outcome, unit, denominator, window and negative control. Technical repeats are not independent biological replicates; no fictional curve supplies a confidence interval or causal proof.\n\n### Limitations and connections\n\nThe exercises are not personal immunity assessments, and they do not prescribe testing or vaccination. Clinical interpretation requires validated assays and current guidance.\n\nConnect the topic to **persistence, latency and chronic infection concepts** on an evidence ladder from molecules to populations. Name the missing assumption before moving between scales, and keep clinical or biosafety decisions outside this teaching model.\n\n### Worked quantitative case 4.3\n\n**Question.** A toy assay shows 90 binding units from 150 measured units. What is the binding fraction?\n\n**Invented inputs:** `b=90`, `t=150`. **Assumed relation:** `b/t`. **Answer:** **0.6 fraction**. Work through the substitution without confusing a percentage with a unitless fraction. State the denominator, and check whether any rate also needs a time unit. A direct ratio answers the arithmetic question but cannot, by itself, establish infectiousness, clinical disease or transmission.\n\n**What might break the inference?** Identify whether a changed sampling frame, a poorly calibrated measurement or an invalid modeling assumption could change the result. Alter the first numerical input by ten percent, keeping other values fixed, and independently recompute the output. Explain whether its response is proportional or nonlinear. All values here are fictional; no measurements or biological replicates were collected.\n\n### Apply the idea\n\n1. Define the central term in **antibodies, lymphocytes and immune memory** and name one tempting but unsupported inference.\n2. State two explanations of the same imaginary observation and propose a distinguishing, safe, noninfectious analysis.\n3. Recalculate the numerical case with correctly labeled units and discuss a limiting case.\n4. Create a three-column evidence ledger: direct observations, inferred mechanism and absent evidence.\n5. Explain how this topic connects to **persistence, latency and chronic infection concepts** while identifying one uncertain link.",
