          "heading": "Topic 7.1 \u2014 Vaccination as immune education",
          "content": "### Mechanism and explanatory scope\n\nVaccines aim to create or reinforce immune memory against selected antigens without requiring natural disease as the route to immune learning. Platforms differ in delivery and context, and outcomes can involve protection against infection, symptomatic disease, severe illness or other endpoints.\n\nFor **immunity and endpoints**, identify the unit of observation, the relevant timescale, and the step for which evidence is missing. A structural observation cannot alone prove a clinical or population-level effect.\n\n### Competing explanations\n\nNo vaccine is automatically sterilizing, and measured effectiveness depends on the outcome, time since immunization, population and circulating pathogens. A correlational comparison can be misleading if groups differ in baseline risk.\n\nCompare a biological change with a shifted sampling frame or instrument response. Connect the topic to **prevention, layered controls and trade-offs** and state one observation that would differentiate the explanations.\n\n### Measurement and controls\n\nInterpret a completely synthetic vaccinated versus unvaccinated cohort table with a clearly named outcome. Calculate risk ratio and relative risk reduction, while noting confounding and why trial designs and observational studies require different safeguards.\n\nDeclare the outcome, unit, denominator, window and negative control. Technical repeats are not independent biological replicates; no fictional curve supplies a confidence interval or causal proof.\n\n### Limitations and connections\n\nThis text makes no recommendations about personal schedules, eligibility or products. Consult qualified health authorities for current advice.\n\nConnect the topic to **immune correlates, waning and uncertainty** on an evidence ladder from molecules to populations. Name the missing assumption before moving between scales, and keep clinical or biosafety decisions outside this teaching model.\n\n### Worked quantitative case 7.1\n\n**Question.** An imagined cohort has outcome risks 0.04 vs 0.10. What is relative risk reduction?\n\n**Invented inputs:** `v=0.04`, `c=0.1`. **Assumed relation:** `1-v/c`. **Answer:** **0.6 fraction**. Work through the substitution without confusing a percentage with a unitless fraction. State the denominator, and check whether any rate also needs a time unit. A direct ratio answers the arithmetic question but cannot, by itself, establish infectiousness, clinical disease or transmission.\n\n**What might break the inference?** Identify whether a changed sampling frame, a poorly calibrated measurement or an invalid modeling assumption could change the result. Alter the first numerical input by ten percent, keeping other values fixed, and independently recompute the output. Explain whether its response is proportional or nonlinear. All values here are fictional; no measurements or biological replicates were collected.\n\n### Apply the idea\n\n1. Define the central term in **vaccination as immune education** and name one tempting but unsupported inference.\n2. State two explanations of the same imaginary observation and propose a distinguishing, safe, noninfectious analysis.\n3. Recalculate the numerical case with correctly labeled units and discuss a limiting case.\n4. Create a three-column evidence ledger: direct observations, inferred mechanism and absent evidence.\n5. Explain how this topic connects to **immune correlates, waning and uncertainty** while identifying one uncertain link.",
