          "heading": "Topic 7.3 \u2014 Antiviral mechanisms and therapeutic evidence",
          "content": "### Mechanism and explanatory scope\n\nAntiviral drugs may interfere with specific viral or host-dependent processes, but their use is justified by controlled evidence for particular indications. Mechanism, exposure at relevant tissues, toxicity, timing and resistance jointly influence outcomes. Pharmacodynamic concepts should not be reduced to a single cell-culture inhibition number.\n\nFor **real-world effectiveness**, identify the unit of observation, the relevant timescale, and the step for which evidence is missing. A structural observation cannot alone prove a clinical or population-level effect.\n\n### Competing explanations\n\nA compound that reduces a reporter signal might affect cell metabolism or assay chemistry rather than viral replication. Laboratory potency does not prove clinical benefit, and outcomes differ across patient populations.\n\nCompare a biological change with a shifted sampling frame or instrument response. Connect the topic to **immune correlates, waning and uncertainty** and state one observation that would differentiate the explanations.\n\n### Measurement and controls\n\nCompare two nonclinical synthetic dose-response curves and annotate effectiveness and viability separately. Identify how randomized comparisons and blinding improve evaluation of treatment claims without making real dosing decisions.\n\nDeclare the outcome, unit, denominator, window and negative control. Technical repeats are not independent biological replicates; no fictional curve supplies a confidence interval or causal proof.\n\n### Limitations and connections\n\nNo drug synthesis, screening workflow, virus manipulation or treatment regimen appears in these exercises. Medication decisions require licensed medical guidance.\n\nConnect the topic to **resistance as an evolutionary and clinical problem** on an evidence ladder from molecules to populations. Name the missing assumption before moving between scales, and keep clinical or biosafety decisions outside this teaching model.\n\n### Worked quantitative case 7.3\n\n**Question.** With marker half-life 6 and duration 12, what fraction remains in a one-phase model?\n\n**Invented inputs:** `t=12`, `h=6`. **Assumed relation:** `2**(-t/h)`. **Answer:** **0.25 fraction**. Work through the substitution without confusing a percentage with a unitless fraction. State the denominator, and check whether any rate also needs a time unit. A direct ratio answers the arithmetic question but cannot, by itself, establish infectiousness, clinical disease or transmission.\n\n**What might break the inference?** Identify whether a changed sampling frame, a poorly calibrated measurement or an invalid modeling assumption could change the result. Alter the first numerical input by ten percent, keeping other values fixed, and independently recompute the output. Explain whether its response is proportional or nonlinear. All values here are fictional; no measurements or biological replicates were collected.\n\n### Apply the idea\n\n1. Define the central term in **antiviral mechanisms and therapeutic evidence** and name one tempting but unsupported inference.\n2. State two explanations of the same imaginary observation and propose a distinguishing, safe, noninfectious analysis.\n3. Recalculate the numerical case with correctly labeled units and discuss a limiting case.\n4. Create a three-column evidence ledger: direct observations, inferred mechanism and absent evidence.\n5. Explain how this topic connects to **resistance as an evolutionary and clinical problem** while identifying one uncertain link.",
