          "content": "### Mechanism and explanatory scope\n\nDrug resistance refers to decreased susceptibility associated with particular biological contexts and validated evidence, whereas clinical nonresponse can arise for many reasons unrelated to resistance. Selection may shape population frequencies, but surveillance and clinical outcomes need separate evaluation.\n\nFor **evolutionary uncertainty**, identify the unit of observation, the relevant timescale, and the step for which evidence is missing. A structural observation cannot alone prove a clinical or population-level effect.\n\n### Competing explanations\n\nAn unusual sequence change does not automatically confer resistance. Treatment history, adherence, diagnosis, host factors, experimental variability and mixed populations complicate interpretation.\n\nCompare a biological change with a shifted sampling frame or instrument response. Connect the topic to **antiviral mechanisms and therapeutic evidence** and state one observation that would differentiate the explanations.\n\n### Measurement and controls\n\nDevelop a synthetic decision tree distinguishing a measured drug-associated marker from a clinical outcome. State which additional validated evidence would be required before using the label resistant.\n\nDeclare the outcome, unit, denominator, window and negative control. Technical repeats are not independent biological replicates; no fictional curve supplies a confidence interval or causal proof.\n\n### Limitations and connections\n\nThe discussion excludes resistance-conferring mutations, engineering routes and optimization. It emphasizes inference and responsible reporting rather than development of evasive variants.\n\nConnect the topic to **prevention, layered controls and trade-offs** on an evidence ladder from molecules to populations. Name the missing assumption before moving between scales, and keep clinical or biosafety decisions outside this teaching model.\n\n### Worked quantitative case 7.4\n\n**Question.** A toy signal drops from 80 to 60. What is the proportional change magnitude?\n\n**Invented inputs:** `a=80`, `b=60`. **Assumed relation:** `(a-b)/a`. **Answer:** **0.25 fraction**. Work through the substitution without confusing a percentage with a unitless fraction. State the denominator, and check whether any rate also needs a time unit. A direct ratio answers the arithmetic question but cannot, by itself, establish infectiousness, clinical disease or transmission.\n\n**What might break the inference?** Identify whether a changed sampling frame, a poorly calibrated measurement or an invalid modeling assumption could change the result. Alter the first numerical input by ten percent, keeping other values fixed, and independently recompute the output. Explain whether its response is proportional or nonlinear. All values here are fictional; no measurements or biological replicates were collected.\n\n### Apply the idea\n\n1. Define the central term in **resistance as an evolutionary and clinical problem** and name one tempting but unsupported inference.\n2. State two explanations of the same imaginary observation and propose a distinguishing, safe, noninfectious analysis.\n3. Recalculate the numerical case with correctly labeled units and discuss a limiting case.\n4. Create a three-column evidence ledger: direct observations, inferred mechanism and absent evidence.\n5. Explain how this topic connects to **prevention, layered controls and trade-offs** while identifying one uncertain link.",
