          "content": "Viruses influence bacterial communities, plants, oceans and biological evolution. Our final unit integrates ecology, molecular evidence, responsible communication and the ethics of open science. Readers should leave able to review a claim, identify what was actually measured and formulate a safer test of competing explanations.\n\nThe five conceptual themes are **microbial communities; environmental scaling; computational attribution; ecosystem feedback; research ethics**. A reader completing this unit should be able to explain each of its five topics, compare at least two rival causes of a given observation, reproduce five quantitative answers, and critique a synthetic research design. Scientific maturity requires distinguishing the scale at which a fact has been measured from the scale at which an explanation is being proposed. If an observation describes nucleic-acid fragments, do not call it an infection count. If an outcome describes an immune-related marker, do not call it proven protection without validation.",
