          "heading": "Topic 8.1 \u2014 Bacteriophages in microbial ecosystems",
          "content": "### Mechanism and explanatory scope\n\nBacteriophages are viruses associated with bacteria and contribute to microbial mortality, gene flow and community dynamics. Their environmental roles depend on host ecology, physiology and spatial structure. Ecological models can describe top-down pressure without requiring technical methods for isolating or modifying phages.\n\nFor **microbial communities**, identify the unit of observation, the relevant timescale, and the step for which evidence is missing. A structural observation cannot alone prove a clinical or population-level effect.\n\n### Competing explanations\n\nA correlation between phage-like particle counts and bacterial abundance does not reveal direction of cause. Increased bacteria may support more phages, while phage-driven mortality can lower specific bacterial populations.\n\nCompare a biological change with a shifted sampling frame or instrument response. Connect the topic to **integrating virology across scales** and state one observation that would differentiate the explanations.\n\n### Measurement and controls\n\nGraph toy bacteria-versus-virus predator\u2013prey trajectories and identify their time-lag assumptions. Discuss what independent ecological measurements would separate host growth, viral predation and environmental covariates.\n\nDeclare the outcome, unit, denominator, window and negative control. Technical repeats are not independent biological replicates; no fictional curve supplies a confidence interval or causal proof.\n\n### Limitations and connections\n\nNo culturing, propagation or genome editing protocols are provided. Ecological teaching models are not recipes for phage therapy or biological control.\n\nConnect the topic to **plant viruses and agricultural systems** on an evidence ladder from molecules to populations. Name the missing assumption before moving between scales, and keep clinical or biosafety decisions outside this teaching model.\n\n### Worked quantitative case 8.1\n\n**Question.** In a toy microcosm table, 45 of 300 particle indices have a particular label. What share is labeled?\n\n**Invented inputs:** `k=45`, `n=300`. **Assumed relation:** `k/n`. **Answer:** **0.15 fraction**. Work through the substitution without confusing a percentage with a unitless fraction. State the denominator, and check whether any rate also needs a time unit. A direct ratio answers the arithmetic question but cannot, by itself, establish infectiousness, clinical disease or transmission.\n\n**What might break the inference?** Identify whether a changed sampling frame, a poorly calibrated measurement or an invalid modeling assumption could change the result. Alter the first numerical input by ten percent, keeping other values fixed, and independently recompute the output. Explain whether its response is proportional or nonlinear. All values here are fictional; no measurements or biological replicates were collected.\n\n### Apply the idea\n\n1. Define the central term in **bacteriophages in microbial ecosystems** and name one tempting but unsupported inference.\n2. State two explanations of the same imaginary observation and propose a distinguishing, safe, noninfectious analysis.\n3. Recalculate the numerical case with correctly labeled units and discuss a limiting case.\n4. Create a three-column evidence ledger: direct observations, inferred mechanism and absent evidence.\n5. Explain how this topic connects to **plant viruses and agricultural systems** while identifying one uncertain link.",
