          "secNumber": "8.8",
          "heading": "Topic 8.5 \u2014 Integrating virology across scales",
          "content": "### Mechanism and explanatory scope\n\nA rigorous explanation can connect virion structure, molecular information flow, host response, ecological opportunity and measurement uncertainty without pretending one scale determines the others. The same observed trend can arise from altered sampling, host behavior, immune state or viral processes.\n\nFor **research ethics**, identify the unit of observation, the relevant timescale, and the step for which evidence is missing. A structural observation cannot alone prove a clinical or population-level effect.\n\n### Competing explanations\n\nA model that explains a single time series may fail under a new sampling policy or ecological context. Elegant diagrams are hypotheses; successful application requires data that separate competing mechanisms and respect relevant denominators.\n\nCompare a biological change with a shifted sampling frame or instrument response. Connect the topic to **viral metagenomics and discovery limits** and state one observation that would differentiate the explanations.\n\n### Measurement and controls\n\nBuild a multi-layer evidence map for an invented community surveillance case. Attach each claim to a measurement type, identify unknown links and propose noninvasive computational falsification tests.\n\nDeclare the outcome, unit, denominator, window and negative control. Technical repeats are not independent biological replicates; no fictional curve supplies a confidence interval or causal proof.\n\n### Limitations and connections\n\nDo not use a classroom integration diagram to infer real pandemic risk, predict a named strain's properties or prescribe interventions. Always separate methods, evidence and decisions.\n\nConnect the topic to **bacteriophages in microbial ecosystems** on an evidence ladder from molecules to populations. Name the missing assumption before moving between scales, and keep clinical or biosafety decisions outside this teaching model.\n\n### Worked quantitative case 8.5\n\n**Question.** A synthetic evidence map has 8 directly measured links out of 20. What share are measured?\n\n**Invented inputs:** `d=8`, `n=20`. **Assumed relation:** `d/n`. **Answer:** **0.4 fraction**. Work through the substitution without confusing a percentage with a unitless fraction. State the denominator, and check whether any rate also needs a time unit. A direct ratio answers the arithmetic question but cannot, by itself, establish infectiousness, clinical disease or transmission.\n\n**What might break the inference?** Identify whether a changed sampling frame, a poorly calibrated measurement or an invalid modeling assumption could change the result. Alter the first numerical input by ten percent, keeping other values fixed, and independently recompute the output. Explain whether its response is proportional or nonlinear. All values here are fictional; no measurements or biological replicates were collected.\n\n### Apply the idea\n\n1. Define the central term in **integrating virology across scales** and name one tempting but unsupported inference.\n2. State two explanations of the same imaginary observation and propose a distinguishing, safe, noninfectious analysis.\n3. Recalculate the numerical case with correctly labeled units and discuss a limiting case.\n4. Create a three-column evidence ledger: direct observations, inferred mechanism and absent evidence.\n5. Explain how this topic connects to **bacteriophages in microbial ecosystems** while identifying one uncertain link.",
